IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `priv51-nouns`
IMPORT `priv51-cross-references`

-- Row il-51. EXPECTED RED: twenty assertions, all failing, two about each of ten defects. The count (20) is declared in check.sh and in encoding.json.
-- Each states what the citing words need. Nothing here is edited to make it pass.
-- Nine defects are citations. In each, the first assertion fails because what the citing words say they point at is not what the target is (the literal reading), and the second fails because the text the citing words
-- name stopped being the text in force on a day long after 15 August 2025. Where a reading through the Interpretation Law section 25 would make the citation reach something in force (P3(b), P5),
-- or where the citing words can be read as the historical text of a Government decision (P1), that reading is recorded in NOTES.md and, for section 25, as a green test in priv51-tests-xrefs.l4. This file does not rule between readings.
-- The tenth defect (P6) is a figure: the notice of 7 October 2026 prints per-person rates that are not multiples of ten shekels, and the literal words of section 23לב(ב) round the rate itself to a multiple of ten.

§ `P1: the First Schedule cites a section that was repealed`

-- 1. The description of the target is not the description of what the citing words point at.
#ASSERT `the citation points at what it says` (`reference: First Schedule item 4(א) to section 7`)
-- 2. The text the citing words name is not in force on a day long after the repeal.
#ASSERT `the named text is in force on` (`reference: First Schedule item 4(א) to section 7`) (Date 10 11 2026) EQUALS JUST TRUE

